--TEST--
`Core\Session::set` encodes its value into a record a store outlives the request holding, so a `secret` is refused there with the same code every other carrier reports — and the help names `setSecret`, because a user's own secret has a sealed door of its own in the session — `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary`, `rule:http-server/a-session-holds-a-secret-only-sealed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary` refuses a `secret` at the one
// recursive graph copy, and `set` makes one: the record it changes is encoded
// to bytes a store holds until the session ends, and nothing on the far side of
// that copy carries the qualifier. The sealed door is the only door, and the
// diagnostic says which one it is rather than offering a reveal that would put
// a plaintext credential in a session store.
class Vault {
    public static function token(): secret string {
        return "sk-live";
    }
}

string $plain = "plain";
secret string $s = "s";

// The accepted side first, so this is a bound with both sides named.
Core\Session::set("cart", $plain);

// Written positionally.
Core\Session::set("refresh", $s);

// Filled by name, which reaches the same parameter — the refusal is read off
// the argument's slot rather than off its position.
Core\Session::set(key: "refresh", value: $s);

// Composed rather than declared: the qualifier poisons through concatenation,
// so a value assembled at the call site reaches the same refusal.
Core\Session::set("refresh", "bearer " . $s);

// Declared one frame away, which is where a rule that only looked at variables
// would miss it.
Core\Session::set("refresh", Vault::token());
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::set` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
   = help: write `setSecret($key, $value, $keys)` instead, which seals the value under a key ring so that what reaches the record is ciphertext; or reveal it explicitly first with `Core\Secret::reveal(..., "reason")`, at the one call site where handing the secret over is the point; a `secret`-typed *property* of a copied object needs nothing here — the walk refuses that one itself
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::set` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::set` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Session::set` encodes it into the record this request writes back to a store that outlives it, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
